        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.093,
            0.672,
            0.117
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 2.3: Let us investigate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.134,
            0.648,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Rinse your mouth with clean water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.158,
            0.883,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Use the blunt end of a clean toothpick, and gently scrape the inside of your cheek."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.2,
            0.883,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Place the scraped material in a drop of water on a clean glass slide and spread it evenly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.243,
            0.884,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Add a drop of methylene blue (a blue-coloured stain) over the material on slide. Adding stain improves the visibility of the material under the microscope by increasing contrast."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.304,
            0.883,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z After one minute, add a drop of glycerin over the material on the slide to prevent the cells from drying."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.346,
            0.883,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Now, carefully place a clean coverslip on the material, and remove the excess glycerin from the edges of the coverslip using blotting paper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.407,
            0.883,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Observe the slide under a microscope and draw what you see in your notebook."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.046,
            0.454,
            0.341,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.046,
                0.454,
                0.341,
                0.605
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.043,
                0.608,
                0.334,
                0.625
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 2.4: Mount of human cheek cells"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.352,
            0.449,
            0.883,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What did you observe? You will observe a polygonshaped structure as shown in Fig. 2.4. These are cheek cells, which form the inner lining of your mouth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.351,
            0.508,
            0.883,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What similarities and differences did you observe between the cells of onion peel in Activity 2.2 and human cheek cells in Activity 2.3?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.351,
            0.565,
            0.883,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have observed that cells have three main parts. The outer layer is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cell membrane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The round structure in the middle is the nucleus, which is also covered by a thin membrane. The space between"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.638,
            0.883,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the cell membrane and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "nucleus",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is filled with "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cytoplasm",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". These three—cell membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus — are the basic parts of a cell. Some cells, like onion peel cells, have an extra outer layer called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cell wall",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". What is the importance of these structures in a cell? What functions do they perform? Are these functions important for the maintenance of life?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.751,
            0.883,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The cell membrane encloses the cytoplasm and nucleus. The cell membrane separates one cell from another. It is porous and allows the entry of materials essential for life processes and the exit of waste material."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.828,
            0.883,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cytoplasm contains other components of the cell and compounds, such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, and mineral salts. Most of the life processes take place within the cytoplasm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
